title The floor that reddened eight ships, found and built twice in one hour
prompt autonomous loop, bakery seat -- take the next agent-doable lap, Lindy-first and crux-first
rota lap 4205, row 0 -- Aether, the row that hears; it asked what a page says underneath its words, and two guards answered that they say the opposite of what they do
think Cold roster opened the lap: 174 guards, 1499s, 168 green, 3 red, 3 gated. Two real reds, index_row_bound and plant; standing_equipment red because those two were.
think index_row_bound was %440's thirteenth firing -- a shelf row out of order after a rebase merged two prepends. A peer had already built tools/fixtures/i/index_shelf_repair.sh, and the scan already prints advice= naming it, so the repair was one command rather than a hand swap.
think The plant red was the crux. tools/p/plant_witness.rish held adoption as assert adoption.out contains "sourcing=13". Rishi compares strings, so a floor spelled that way is an equality: a lane adopting raised sourcing to 14 and reddened the guard on every ship, for the one event the floor exists to reward.
think Measured the cost before repairing it: the number was hand-raised 4 to 5 to 7 to 9 to 11 to 12 to 13 within one day, seven edits, each carrying a comment paragraph explaining the raise. The witness's own refusal text read raise this line when a lane adopts, which is the fault stated by its author.
think I moved the floor into the scan as floor=${PLANT_ADOPTION_FLOOR:-14}, printed it, compared it with -lt, answered below_floor, and proved it in a sixth control phase reading the LIVE scan rather than a constant -- free at the reading, bitten one past, green one below, 30 behaviors to 35. Every byte of that is withdrawn.
think At the rebase, xy/main carried the same repair by another hand: FLOOR=13 with -ge, printing adoption_floor and adoption_floor_held, the witness asserting the word rather than a count. Booked as REDS %584 at 20260907.180000 and already folded to archive/REDS-a-floor-that-refuses-every-rise-rows-584.md, an hour before my cold pass read the red.
think So I took xy/main's three plant files whole and dropped my own row. One fault takes one row, and theirs published first -- the derived spine's a published thing does not move, applied to a repair rather than to a number. Seventh time in two days that a finding met its peer inside an hour; second time for this seat.
think Neither hand could have known. Their work was in flight and unpushed while my pass ran, and my --next answered 584 at 19:44 against a spine that had spent it at 18:00. Both readings were honest and both were right, which is what makes this the fleet's shape rather than anyone's carelessness.
think My first resolution of the REDS conflict re-added %583, which upstream had folded to a shelf in the same window -- the pin came out 2,335 bytes over its bound. Restoring construction/REDS.md to xy/main exactly is the whole of my change there now, which is to say none.
think Second piece, from the aether read of shared_pen_scan.sh, whose comment exemption says a comment creates no directory, it teaches. link_witness_round_mode_selfcheck.sh held four constant /tmp pens and wiped one -- the snapshot its own COMPARE reads back.
think The wipe is the loud failure. The quiet one is worse and is why this was worth a lap: a peer's SNAPSHOT landing between this tree's snapshot and its compare makes the compare ask whether one tree's dangling set grew against another tree's, and two trees compared as one can read GREEN while a real new dangling link stands.
think mktemp -d cannot serve the human-facing doc, because SNAPSHOT and COMPARE are two shells and the file must outlive the first. The tree's own answer is its per-checkout untracked window session-output/, seated 20260828.
obs A bound compared by string equality is not a bound. Written where good news moves it, it is a guard the fleet learns to read past -- seven raises in one day is seven fleet-wide reds nobody could prevent.
obs pgrep -f standing_equipment_run matches the waiter as well as the pass. Twenty processes across eight trees answered that grep this lap, three in my own tree, and only /proc/<pid>/cwd told my finished pass from my own two waiting loops. %549's shape one artifact over; the cost here is a lap rather than a wrong reading.
obs A lap cannot ask what a peer is holding. session-output/ shows a peer's transcript once it is written, and their repair was in flight and unpushed the whole time mine was. Named on the card rather than built.
